\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Hourly total load and a centered seven-day descriptive mean (F01). Summer changes the load level and amplitude. December is shaded; this whole-year plot is descriptive, and its centered mean is not a model feature.}}{5}{figure.caption.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Daily means of the three distribution-zone loads (F02). Their differing seasonal behavior contributes to the changing city total. December is shown for description, not as a development validation fold.}}{6}{figure.caption.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Mean daily profiles by season, January--November only (F03). The common cycle motivates hour encodings; changes in level motivate historical load predictors. The chart does not isolate temperature or daylight effects.}}{6}{figure.caption.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Development-period hour--weekday patterns (F04). The right panel expresses deviations relative to the weekday mean at the same hour, revealing variation otherwise dominated by the daily load cycle.}}{7}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Ramadan and pre-Ramadan hourly profiles, with listed public holidays removed (F05). Large nighttime differences motivate a special-period indicator; seasonal confounding remains.}}{7}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Target-hour temperature and total load, colored by time of day (F06a). Distinct bands indicate that calendar conditioning is relevant when interpreting the weather--load relationship.}}{8}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Pearson correlations on hourly development data (F06b). Strong correlations between zones and their sum are expected arithmetically; contemporaneous zones are excluded as forecasting inputs.}}{8}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Autocorrelation of the hourly total through 200 lags, January--November (F07). Daily and weekly dependence motivate history-based predictors and make randomly shuffled evaluation a poor proxy for future forecasting.}}{9}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Chronological partitions and five expanding validation folds (F10). December is outside model-selection folds. The original graphic's ``untouched'' wording describes the intended partition; whole-year descriptive EDA and later test reruns qualify literal unseen-data claims.}}{13}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces November h24 RMSE and MAPE for seasonal baselines, default regressors and RF-P1 (F11). The vertical references mark Naive-24h. Rankings and baseline improvements apply to this validation month.}}{15}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Actual load, default XGB forecasts and Naive-24h during 13--19 November (F11b). XGB was selected for this illustration by November validation RMSE. The week is part of model development and is not the December test.}}{16}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Default h24 RMSE across expanding validation folds (F12). Fold 0 covers 4 July--2 August and reveals substantial sensitivity of several nonlinear estimators to the summer period. MeanProfile is omitted from the graphic but retained in the numerical tables.}}{18}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces Default, tuned absolute and tuned residual h24 CV RMSE by model family (F13). Error bars are sample SDs over five temporal folds. Bar percentages compare against the absolute default, so residual improvements include both tuning and target reformulation.}}{22}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces RF/XGB h24 feature ablation with D-selected hyperparameters held fixed (F15). Error bars show fold sample SD. Residual models begin at D because they require lag-24; F removes weather from E. These are conditional predictive comparisons.}}{23}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces Development CV RMSE for separately tuned h24 and h1 family representatives (F15b). Each bar uses the better target formulation within its family and setting. The h24 label denotes the daily-batch/minimum-lag convention explained in \cref {sec:features}, rather than a uniform 24-hour issuance lead.}}{25}{figure.caption.28}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces Actual and selected h24 SVR test forecasts, with an 11--17 December zoom and the daily naive reference (F17). Measurements from completed prior days update the load-history inputs throughout the month.}}{28}{figure.caption.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17}{\ignorespaces Selected h24 SVR errors over December and their empirical distribution (F17b). Negative errors indicate underprediction. The early-month negative tail warrants attention despite the small average bias.}}{28}{figure.caption.32}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18}{\ignorespaces December MAE by hour and weekday for selected h24 SVR and Naive-24h (F18). Holidays are separated from weekdays; the holiday group contains only 1 December, limiting generalization of that comparison.}}{29}{figure.caption.33}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {19}{\ignorespaces Residual-RF built-in and test permutation importance (F19a). Built-in importance concerns the correction to lag-24; permutation importance concerns the full forecast. Shuffling lag-24 also changes the explicit forecast base, so the two panels measure different quantities.}}{29}{figure.caption.35}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {20}{\ignorespaces Tree SHAP summary for the residual RF on December inputs (F19b). Contributions are in kW of the learned correction, not the complete load or the selected SVR output.}}{30}{figure.caption.36}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {21}{\ignorespaces RF diagnostic results under four evaluation settings (F16). The original image title attributes score inflation to leakage; the experiment changes split, features, target resolution and forecast horizon, so the bars do not isolate a leakage effect.}}{32}{figure.caption.38}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {22}{\ignorespaces Zone and total-load behavior around 20 April (DQ01). Redistribution between networks is a plausible interpretation; no independent operational log confirms it.}}{43}{figure.caption.44}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {23}{\ignorespaces Whole-year wind-speed diagnostic (DQ02). The bimodal distribution motivates caution about sensor semantics; the pipeline retains the observed values. This descriptive graphic includes December.}}{43}{figure.caption.45}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {24}{\ignorespaces Development-period weekday and holiday load profiles (F08). Group differences motivate calendar predictors but are neither causal effects nor independently validated event forecasts.}}{43}{figure.caption.46}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {25}{\ignorespaces Selected h24 SVR forecasts against actual December loads (F17c). The equality reference aids assessment of large local misses; proximity alone does not establish calibrated uncertainty or temporal independence.}}{44}{figure.caption.47}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {26}{\ignorespaces Residual-RF Tree SHAP dependence plots for temperature and lag-24 on December inputs (F19c). Values are contributions to the residual model output in kW, not to the separately added baseline.}}{45}{figure.caption.48}%
